        <mxCell id="300" value="4 · KEY TRADE-OFFS (choice vs named alternative vs what is given up)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#2D6A2D;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3420" width="700" height="30" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="301" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Runner PULLs (long-poll) vs control plane PUSHing&lt;/b&gt; — self-hosted runners sit behind customer firewalls with no inbound path, so push is structurally impossible for a large slice of the fleet. &lt;i&gt;Given up:&lt;/i&gt; a poll interval of scheduling latency (mitigated by long-polling, not tight polling) and 50k idle HTTP connections to hold.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fresh microVM per job vs container per job vs reusing a machine&lt;/b&gt; — the workload is arbitrary untrusted code holding repo secrets; a container shares a kernel, so a breakout reaches the next tenant's secrets. Firecracker gives VM-grade isolation at ~125ms boot. &lt;i&gt;Given up:&lt;/i&gt; utilisation and cost — reuse would be far cheaper, and that trade is exactly what the security requirement forbids.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Warm pool vs cold boot on demand&lt;/b&gt; — a cold, fully-provisioned runner image takes 30–60s; the &amp;lt;1s scheduling NFR is unreachable without pre-booted capacity. &lt;i&gt;Given up:&lt;/i&gt; you pay for idle VMs, and pool sizing becomes a forecasting problem with a real cost curve.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Durable orchestrator (DTF / Temporal) vs cron-polling the Run Store for ready jobs&lt;/b&gt; — a job DAG spanning hours with retries and fan-in is exactly a durable workflow; polling re-implements it badly and scans the whole table. &lt;i&gt;Given up:&lt;/i&gt; a heavy stateful dependency on the critical path, plus its own scaling story.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;SSE vs WebSockets vs polling for logs&lt;/b&gt; — traffic is server→client only, so a WebSocket buys a bidirectional channel you never use and costs a stateful connection per viewer; polling at 500ms freshness would hammer the API. &lt;i&gt;Given up:&lt;/i&gt; no client→server channel (fine — cancel piggybacks on the runner heartbeat) and SSE's 6-connection-per-domain limit on HTTP/1.1.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Postgres sharded by repoId vs a single primary vs Cassandra&lt;/b&gt; — run/job state needs transactional DAG-state transitions, so a wide-column store fights you; a single primary dies on write volume + the artifact/log index. &lt;i&gt;Given up:&lt;/i&gt; cross-repo queries (fine — those are the analytics that were scoped out) and resharding pain.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Per-org fairness in the broker vs FIFO queueing&lt;/b&gt; — FIFO lets one monorepo's 200-job matrix occupy the whole pool and starve every other customer. &lt;i&gt;Given up:&lt;/i&gt; the broker becomes stateful and more complex than a queue pop, and heavy users see queueing they would not under FIFO.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#FFF2CC;strokeColor=#D6B656;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3454" width="1660" height="360" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="400" value="5 · DEEP DIVES" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#2D6A2D;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3850" width="500" height="30" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="401" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;A. THE SCALE BREAK — boot latency, not machine count&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;50k concurrent VMs is a procurement problem, not a design problem. The real break is &lt;b&gt;&amp;quot;fresh machine&amp;quot; vs &amp;quot;&amp;lt;1s scheduling&amp;quot;&lt;/b&gt; — a cold runner image boots in 30–60s, so the NFR is unmeetable by construction. Fix: a warm pool of pre-booted microVMs per (osImage, region), claimed in milliseconds.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The pool then owns the hard problem: &lt;b&gt;sizing against a spiky, long-tailed workload&lt;/b&gt;. CI traffic is brutally diurnal (a 5× working-hours spike) and job duration is p50 2 min / p99 1 hr, so a 5-min mean badly under-predicts occupancy. Size the pool on forecast + a burst headroom of ~2 min of peak arrival rate; when it drains, degrade to the cold path and let scheduling latency rise rather than dropping jobs. &lt;b&gt;If the traffic estimate is off by 10×&lt;/b&gt;, nothing structural changes — the pool target and the shard count move, and cost moves linearly; that is the mark of the right decomposition.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;B. IDEMPOTENCY UNDER AT-LEAST-ONCE (three separate places)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;1. &lt;i&gt;Webhook redelivery&lt;/i&gt; — GitHub retries. Without dedupe on X-GitHub-Delivery you double the fleet cost and post two conflicting statuses on one commit. UNIQUE constraint on deliveryId; the redelivery returns the existing runId.&lt;br/&gt;2. &lt;i&gt;Job re-execution after a lost heartbeat&lt;/i&gt; — the runner may still be alive and mid-deploy. Leases with fencing: the requeue bumps the lease generation, and the zombie runner's /complete is rejected because its leaseId is stale. This is the write-skew trap of the whole system.&lt;br/&gt;3. &lt;i&gt;Log batch retries&lt;/i&gt; — monotonic seqNo per jobRun makes a replayed batch a no-op and makes gaps detectable.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;C. HOT PARTITION / HOT TENANT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Partitioning raw_events by repoId means one enormous monorepo pins one Kafka partition — a hot key you cannot hash away, since ordering per repo is what you wanted. Mitigate by keying on (repoId, workflowId) and accepting cross-workflow reordering, which the DAG does not depend on. On the compute side the same tenant starves the pool: per-org concurrency quota enforced in the broker, with queued-position surfaced in the API so the user sees &lt;i&gt;why&lt;/i&gt; they are waiting rather than assuming the system is down.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;D. CONSISTENCY MODEL&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Job state transitions are strongly consistent (single-row Postgres transactions on JobRun, guarded by lease generation) — a job must never be running twice. Everything read-side is eventually consistent and should be: run lists, log tails, and the GitHub check status all tolerate a second of staleness. The one place users notice is the check status, so the Status Reporter upserts on checkRunId and is safe to replay.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;E. OPERABILITY&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;• &lt;i&gt;Primary alert is queue depth per label&lt;/i&gt;, not CPU — a rising ubuntu-22.04 backlog is the leading indicator of every user-visible failure here.&lt;br/&gt;• &lt;i&gt;&amp;quot;How do I know logs are stale?&amp;quot;&lt;/i&gt; — a synthetic canary job every minute that emits a timestamped line; alert on observed end-to-end log latency, never on the pipeline's own health metrics.&lt;br/&gt;• &lt;i&gt;The job that never ends&lt;/i&gt; — hard timeoutMinutes per job plus a reaper sweeping expired leases; without it the p99 tail permanently erodes the pool.&lt;br/&gt;• &lt;i&gt;Poison workflow&lt;/i&gt; — a plan that fails to parse or crashes the planner must go to a DLQ with the run marked failed, not retried forever against the same Kafka offset.&lt;br/&gt;• &lt;i&gt;Cost&lt;/i&gt; — the fleet dominates: ~50k VMs at peak, and warm-pool idle time is the line item to watch. Artifacts at ~0.5 GB/s with 7-day retention is ~300 TB resident — the S3 lifecycle rule is load-bearing, not housekeeping.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#D5E8D4;strokeColor=#82B366;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3884" width="1660" height="640" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
